    "description": "Iran's point-of-sale equipment sector includes documented domestic assembly alongside equipment supply and support. A factory report on Tosan Techno illustrates why assembly, component production, and plans for deeper localization must be distinguished.",
    "tags": ["Point of sale", "Manufacturing", "Payment terminals", "Technology transfer", "Industrial development"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:34:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:34:41Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Point-of-Sale Manufacturing in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore evidence of payment-terminal assembly in Iran and the distinction between completed manufacturing activities and localization plans."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "manufacturing-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What domestic production means",
        "content": "The term “Iranian card reader” needs careful interpretation. A factory visit published in Solar Hijri year 1397 described Tosan Techno assembling PAX payment terminals at the Caspian industrial estate near Qazvin. The reported work included assembling components, testing, quality control, and packaging. This is evidence of an operating assembly process at a particular site and time; it does not establish that every component was made domestically. [s1]\n\nThe same account separated that observed activity from proposed later stages. Company management described plans to produce terminal housings and subsequently undertake printed-circuit-board production and surface-mount assembly, in which electronic components are mounted onto boards. Those stages were presented as future developments. The report cannot establish their eventual completion. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "production-stages",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Observed assembly and proposed localization stages",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"PAX terminal components\"] -->|\"Observed assembly\"| B[\"Assembled terminals\"]\n  B -->|\"Observed testing\"| C[\"Quality control\"]\n  C -->|\"Observed finishing stage\"| D[\"Packaging\"]\n  E[\"Proposed housing production\"] -->|\"Planned deeper localization\"| F[\"Proposed circuit-board production and surface-mount assembly\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The upper chain shows activities described during the factory visit. The separate lower chain shows announced localization stages whose completion is not established by that report.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "equipment-and-service",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Hardware and the service relationship",
        "content": "Equipment supply also has a merchant-facing dimension. SEP's terminal catalogue distinguishes Wi-Fi, computer-connected, wired-network, Android, and cellular-connected devices. These categories describe different combinations of connectivity, software platform, and integration; they should not be read as a list of domestic manufacturers. [s2]\n\nThe catalogue also presents installation, support, and online reporting as parts of the terminal offering. For an international reader, that distinction matters: the business presenting a card reader to a merchant may be delivering payment acceptance and maintenance rather than documenting the machine's manufacturing origin. A product catalogue supports statements about offered equipment and services, but does not independently prove component provenance or production depth. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "industrial-reading",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A measured reading of localization",
        "content": "The factory evidence supports a specific conclusion: domestic assembly and associated quality-control work formed part of this company's payment-terminal activity. It also shows that management treated localization as a sequence of industrial steps. Assembly, housing production, and circuit-board work therefore need separate evidence rather than a single undifferentiated “made locally” label. [s1]\n\nA current industry account requires newer factory documentation, product-origin records, and comparable output figures. Until those are inspected, the historical example should remain a bounded case study rather than a claim about the entire Iranian market.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
